```

I would **not vendor any code into Pāṭala itself yet**.

First run an automated repo-mining pass producing:

```text
EXTERNAL-REUSE-MATRIX.jsonl
```

with one record per interesting subsystem:

```json
{
  "repo": "dharmamitra/mitra-parallel",
  "asset": "sentence alignments",
  "patala_target": "TranslationParallel",
  "reuse": "ingest",
  "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0",
  "integration_cost": "low",
  "value": "very_high"
}
```

Then the best architecture becomes strikingly clear:

```text
                   WHAT OTHERS ALREADY BUILT

 PANDiT ─────────────── bibliographic universe
 Pāṇḍitya ───────────── PANDiT ETL
 GRETIL fixes ───────── textual normalization
 BuddhaNexus ────────── segmentation + parallels
 DCS ────────────────── corpus morphology
 DCS↔Heritage ───────── analyzer disagreement
 Heritage.py ────────── parser API
 Vidyut ─────────────── Sanskrit engine
 MITRA ──────────────── cross-language alignment
 pramana-nlp ────────── philosophy corpus ETL
 FoJin ──────────────── hundreds-source ingestion
 CSL ────────────────── lexical evidence
 SARIT ──────────────── editions/witnesses

                         ↓↓↓

                     PĀṬALA

            assertion/evidence binding
                    argument graph
                     crux graph
               epistemic-strength gates
                 translation decisions
                  review/adjudication
                proof-of-scholarship
               educational derivation
```

That's the important strategic result: **the bottom 60–70% of Pāṭala increasingly looks assemblable from obscure existing work.**

The part nobody seems to have built is the top layer that turns all these heterogeneous philological signals into **reviewable claims, arguments, scholarly judgements and proofs of understanding**. That's where almost all custom engineering effort should go.

